            <p class="section-label">Static Fire Test 1</p>
            <div class="content-block">
                <p>
                    <strong>Setup:</strong> restrained 2-DOF gimbal test stand,
                    pivot at approximately vehicle CG, sized to hold the F15-0's
                    thrust. A custom low-resistance test-stand gimbal — separate
                    from the flight TVC unit — clamps a 4" body tube on the
                    inner structure and bolts to a shelving frame on the outer
                    structure, modeled through a Python script imported into NX.
                </p>
                <figure class="image-figure">
                    <div class="image-placeholder">
                        <img
                            src="/images/tvc-rocket/06-test-stand-isolated.jpeg"
                            alt="The isolated test stand and gimbal mount"
                            loading="lazy"
                        />
                        <p class="caption">The isolated test stand and gimbal mount. Note the lean to one side, suspected at the time to be an imbalance from the avionics bay sitting off-center.</p>
                    </div>
                </figure>
                <p>
                    <strong>What happened:</strong> ignition was clean, but the
                    vehicle rotated roughly 80° from vertical within 0.7 s and
                    struck the stand's mechanical stop, rebounding and staying
                    displaced for the remaining ~2.7 s of the burn. From the
                    outside, the gimbal looked dead.
                </p>
                <figure class="image-figure image-figure--narrow">
                    <div class="image-placeholder video-placeholder">
                        <video
                            src="/images/tvc-rocket/04-static-fire-test1.mp4"
                            controls
                            playsinline
                            preload="metadata"
                        ></video>
                        <p class="caption">Test 1 — the rocket rotates into the mechanical stop within the first second and stays there for the rest of the burn.</p>
                    </div>
                </figure>
                <p>
                    <strong>What the data actually showed:</strong> the log ran
                    clean — 278 samples at the 83 Hz burn-window rate, zero
                    dropouts over 50 ms, thrust tracking the certified F15-0
                    curve almost exactly (peak thrust fraction 0.997). The loop
                    runs at 66.7 Hz before ignition and switches to 83.3 Hz when
                    <code>motorIgnited</code> latches, in both tests — a ~25%
                    loop-rate change at the exact moment control authority starts
                    to matter. But the pre-ignition attitude read
                    +78.8° pitch and +109.5° roll, when video confirmed the
                    vehicle was within about 1° of plumb before ignition. That
                    phantom 78° error was enough to clamp the PID output on the
                    very first sample, and it never recovered — both servo
                    channels sat at a fixed extreme for the entire burn (inner
                    servo used only values 86/94, outer only 96/104; each axis
                    spent 100% of the burn at a ±4° extreme, with only 5–8
                    command changes total across 273 samples).
                </p>
                <figure class="image-figure image-figure--data">
                    <div class="image-placeholder">
                        <img
                            src="/images/tvc-rocket/01_test1_full_session.svg"
                            alt="Line chart of pitch and roll over 95 seconds. Both traces are flat near zero until 2.55 seconds before ignition, when they abruptly diverge, pitch climbing to 80 degrees and roll sweeping across the full plus/minus 180 degree range."
                            loading="lazy"
                        />
                        <p class="caption">
                            Attitude estimate across the entire Test 1 log. The
                            estimate holds within 0.12° standard deviation for
                            95 seconds, then diverges at t = −2.55 s — 2.55 s
                            before <code>motorIgnited</code> latches, and 1.55 s
                            before the motor actually lit. Frame-by-frame
                            tracking of the test video puts the airframe
                            motionless to within about half a degree across that
                            whole window, while the estimate read 79° in pitch
                            and swept roll across nearly the full ±180°
                            <code>atan2</code> output range. Attitude shown is
                            the complementary-filter output, not raw
                            accelerometer angle.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </figure>
                <figure class="image-figure image-figure--data">
                    <div class="image-placeholder">
                        <img
                            src="/images/tvc-rocket/02_test1_attitude_and_servo.svg"
                            alt="Two stacked charts sharing a time axis. The upper shows pitch and roll diverging through the burn. The lower shows both servo commands as flat steps pinned at their extreme values, almost never changing."
                            loading="lazy"
                        />
                        <p class="caption">
                            Burn-window detail. Both servo channels sat at a
                            travel limit for 100% of the burn — the inner axis
                            used 2 of its 9 addressable positions, the outer 2
                            of 9, with 4 and 7 command changes across 274
                            samples. Grey lines mark the 9 integer positions
                            <code>write()</code> can address. These are
                            commanded positions; there is no position feedback
                            in the system, so the log cannot confirm the servo
                            reached the angle.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </figure>
                <p>
                    The root cause was a one-line bug: the roll computation used
                    <code>atan2(a.y, a.z)</code>, which assumes Z is the
                    vertical axis. In this build, Y is the longitudinal axis, so
                    with gravity resting on Y the function was handed two
                    near-zero channels and its output ran noise-dominated across
                    nearly the full ±180° range — logged roll swept −176.67° to
                    +176.61° on a bolted-down, motionless vehicle, starting
                    1.55 s before the motor lit. No thrust or motion was
                    involved. Several explanations seemed possible at first, but
                    the axis-convention framing was the right one — and it is the
                    more serious finding.
                </p>
                <p>
                    The key piece of evidence came from frame-by-frame video
                    analysis (446 frames, ignition at t = 8.43 s): the exhaust
                    plume was visibly vectored about 15° off the airframe axis
                    for the whole burn, and the direction of that deflection
                    matched the direction of the observed rotation. The gimbal
                    wasn't inactive — it <em>caused</em> the rotation, holding a
                    hard-over deflection commanded by a sensor reporting an
                    attitude error that didn't exist.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Fixes applied before Test 2: corrected <code>atan2</code>
                    argument order, removed a <code>motorIgnited = true</code>
                    at boot that had been bypassing real ignition detection,
                    added complementary-filter gyro fusion (α = 0.98), added
                    boot-time gyro bias calibration, added a deployment-inhibit
                    flag, and raised the gain-schedule floor from 0.05 to 0.4
                    (capping PID amplification at 2.5× instead of 20×).
                    Hands-off bench validation after the mounting fix held pitch
                    −0.10° / roll +6.45° with 0.09° standard deviation over
                    30 s — confirming the sensor fix before spending another
                    motor.
                </p>
                <p>
                    A second, independent bug sat in the same log: ignition
                    detection ran roughly 1.0 s late. The servos held dead
                    center for 6,501 consecutive samples and moved only when the
                    flag latched, so for the first second of the burn the
                    vehicle was rotating under uncorrected thrust misalignment
                    while the flight computer had not yet registered that the
                    motor was lit. The control loop was not failing — it was not
                    running. Everything keyed to elapsed-time-since-ignition,
                    including the burn timer and the burnout call, was running on
                    a clock short by about a quarter of the burn. Timing was
                    established by aligning the test video's acoustic ignition
                    onset (8.23 s) against the first commanded gimbal deflection
                    (9.23 s), both at 30 fps.
                </p>
            </div>

            <p class="section-label">Static Fire Test 2</p>
            <div class="content-block">
                <figure class="image-figure image-figure--narrow">
                    <div class="image-placeholder">
                        <img
                            src="/images/tvc-rocket/05-initial-setup-tilt.jpeg"
                            alt="Rig setup ahead of Test 2"
                            loading="lazy"
