                    </div>
                </figure>
                <p>
                    <strong>Setup:</strong> all Test 1 fixes live — real
                    ignition detection, gyro-fused attitude, deployment
                    inhibited and logged but not actuated. Pre-ignition attitude
                    held pitch −1.75° (σ 0.14) and roll −8.92° (σ 0.21) for
                    the three seconds before ignition, both servos parked at
                    center, body rates under 0.4°/s. Clean setup.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong>What happened:</strong> at ignition the vehicle
                    diverged immediately and monotonically, reaching −307° in
                    pitch within 2.5 s.
                </p>
                <figure class="image-figure image-figure--data">
                    <div class="image-placeholder">
                        <img
                            src="/images/tvc-rocket/03_test2_baseline_and_divergence.svg"
                            alt="Line chart showing pitch and roll flat and quiet for 50 seconds before ignition, then pitch dropping steeply and continuously to negative 307 degrees within two and a half seconds."
                            loading="lazy"
                        />
                        <p class="caption">
                            Test 2 held a clean pre-ignition baseline — pitch
                            −1.75° (σ 0.14), roll −8.92° (σ 0.21) over the three
                            seconds before ignition, body rates under 0.4°/s. At
                            ignition the vehicle diverged immediately and
                            monotonically, reaching −307° in pitch by
                            t = 2.52 s. Pitch here is unbounded gyro
                            integration, not a wrapped orientation reading.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </figure>
                <figure class="image-figure image-figure--narrow">
                    <div class="image-placeholder video-placeholder">
                        <video
                            src="/images/tvc-rocket/10-static-fire-test2.mp4"
                            controls
                            playsinline
                            preload="metadata"
                        ></video>
                        <p class="caption">Test 2 — clean ignition, then immediate divergence.</p>
                    </div>
                </figure>
                <p>
                    <strong>What the data actually showed:</strong> this was a
                    fully different failure from Test 1, and it's the more
                    interesting one — Test 2 is the first data set that actually
                    closes the loop, recording what the vehicle did in response
                    to a known command. Both control signs were inverted. At
                    every sampled point in the first 0.9 s, a command to
                    increase pitch produced a pitch that decreased instead —
                    100% of the pitch axis and 9 of 11 sampled roll points moved
                    opposite to the commanded correction. That's positive
                    feedback: every correction the controller made enlarged the
                    error instead of shrinking it, so it saturated within about
                    two loop iterations and stayed pinned there (98.9% / 96.3%
                    time-at-extreme on inner/outer). The measured angular
                    acceleration, −567°/s², was the same order of magnitude as
                    the ≈330°/s² measured optically in Test 1.
                </p>
                <figure class="image-figure image-figure--data">
                    <p class="figure-flag">Key evidence — inverted control sign</p>
                    <div class="image-placeholder">
                        <img
                            src="/images/tvc-rocket/05_test2_saturation_vs_divergence.svg"
                            alt="Two stacked charts. The upper shows the inner servo command flat at its minimum for nearly the whole window. The lower shows pitch falling smoothly and continuously from near zero to negative 195 degrees over the same window."
                            loading="lazy"
                        />
                        <p class="caption">
                            The clearest evidence for the inverted control sign.
                            Over the first 0.9 s after ignition the inner servo
                            was held at its −4° rail for 70 of 71 samples — the
                            maximum correction available to the controller —
                            while pitch ran monotonically from −3.25° to
                            −195.4°, passing −45° at 0.35 s, −90° at 0.51 s and
                            −180° at 0.80 s. A correctly-signed loop saturating
                            at a rail drives error toward zero. This one grew
                            error the entire time.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </figure>
                <p class="figure-note">
                    A command-versus-response correlation was considered and
                    rejected as a method here. In a closed loop the controller
                    responds to error and the plant responds to command, so
                    anticorrelation between the two appears whether or not the
                    sign is inverted. The saturation-with-growing-error argument
                    does not have that problem.
                </p>
                <figure class="image-figure image-figure--data">
                    <div class="image-placeholder">
                        <img
                            src="/images/tvc-rocket/04_test2_attitude_and_servo.svg"
                            alt="Two stacked charts. The upper shows pitch falling steeply through the burn. The lower shows servo commands stepping between discrete integer levels but spending nearly all the time at the extremes."
                            loading="lazy"
                        />
                        <p class="caption">
                            Burn-window detail for Test 2. The inner channel
                            spent 98.9% and the outer 96.4% of the burn at a
                            travel limit, using 5 and 7 of the 9 available
                            positions. The saturation signature is nearly
                            identical to Test 1 despite an unrelated root cause.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </figure>
                <p>
                    The reason bench testing didn't catch this: earlier
                    servo-direction checks confirmed which way each
                    <em>servo</em> moves for a given command. But loop sign
                    depends on which way the <em>vehicle</em> rotates in
                    response to the resulting nozzle deflection — a different
                    question entirely, and correctness on the first doesn't
                    imply correctness on the second.
                </p>
                <p>
                    A secondary finding came out of the deployment logic, and it
                    showed up in both tests: accelerometer dead-reckoning
                    produced roughly −53 m/s of apparent vertical velocity by
                    burnout — −52.5 m/s in Test 2, −53.6 m/s in Test 1 — on a
                    vehicle that never physically moved, and in both runs the
                    velocity-based deployment trigger fired 0.03 s after the
                    arming delay expired. In an actual flight this logic would
                    have deployed the parachute at burnout, at max velocity.
                    <code>DEPLOYMENT_INHIBITED</code> correctly suppressed the
                    actuation, but the finding rules out accelerometer
                    integration as a deployment trigger; a barometric altimeter
                    is required going forward.
                </p>
                <figure class="image-figure image-figure--data">
                    <div class="image-placeholder">
                        <img
                            src="/images/tvc-rocket/08_deadreckoned_velocity.svg"
                            alt="Line chart of dead-reckoned vertical velocity for both tests, each falling steadily to about negative 53 metres per second by burnout, with markers showing where the deployment trigger fired."
                            loading="lazy"
                        />
                        <p class="caption">
                            Accelerometer integration produced roughly −53 m/s
                            of apparent vertical velocity by burnout in both
                            tests, on a vehicle bolted to a stand that never
                            physically moved. The velocity-based deployment
                            trigger fired 0.03 s after the arming delay expired
                            in both runs — in flight this logic would have
                            deployed the parachute at burnout, at maximum
                            velocity. <code>DEPLOYMENT_INHIBITED</code> correctly
                            suppressed actuation, but the finding rules out
                            accelerometer integration as a deployment trigger. A
                            barometric altimeter is required.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </figure>
                <p>
                    There's also a resolution problem sitting underneath the sign
                    bug: the gimbal linkage has a measured 2.3× servo-to-gimbal
                    ratio, and with <code>PWMServo.write()</code> restricted to
                    integer degrees, the entire ±10° gimbal range collapses onto
                    about <strong>9 discrete servo positions, roughly 2.6° apart</strong>.
                    That's coarser than the attitude error the controller is
                    trying to null — so even with the sign corrected, the system
                    can't finely settle near zero error. This is why both tests
                    show the same saturation signature despite having two
                    unrelated root causes: saturation looks the same on the
                    outside whether it comes from a false 78° reference or an
                    inverted sign, because in both cases the controller is
                    working against a resolution and range problem it can't
                    escape.
                </p>
                <figure class="image-figure image-figure--data">
                    <div class="image-placeholder">
                        <img
                            src="/images/tvc-rocket/06_quantizer_usage.svg"
                            alt="Two horizontal bar charts side by side, one per test, showing the percentage of burn samples spent at each commanded servo position. Nearly all bars cluster at the extreme positions with almost nothing in between."
                            loading="lazy"
                        />
                        <p class="caption">
                            Distribution of commanded servo positions during
                            each burn. The 2.3× servo-to-gimbal linkage ratio
                            combined with <code>write()</code>'s integer-degree
                            argument collapses the ±10° gimbal range onto 9
                            addressable positions roughly 2.6° apart — coarser
                            than the attitude error the controller is trying to
                            null. Neither test used more than a fraction of
                            them; both lived at the rails.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </figure>
                <figure class="image-figure image-figure--narrow">
                    <div class="image-placeholder">
                        <img
                            src="/images/tvc-rocket/11-test-stand-broken.jpeg"
                            alt="The test stand gimbal after Test 2"
                            loading="lazy"
                        />
                        <p class="caption">The test stand gimbal after Test 2 — didn't survive a second hard-over event.</p>
                    </div>
                </figure>
                <p>
                    <strong>Hypotheses ruled out:</strong> lateral CG offset from
                    the off-center avionics bay — ruled out; the vehicle sat
                    motionless within ~1° of plumb for 8 seconds before Test 1
                    ignition, and the resting lean turned out to be pivot
                    friction (not repeatable session-to-session). Servo thermal
                    damage from a prolonged stall — ruled out; Test 2 showed
                    normal servo response. Gyro clipping under vibration — not
                    the primary cause (the stand allows free rotation and the
                    numbers match Test 1's independent optical measurement), but
                    the gyro range was raised to ±2000°/s regardless, and raw
                    accelerometer angle and gyro rate are now logged separately
                    so real rotation and sensor saturation can be told apart next
                    time.
                </p>
            </div>

            <p class="section-label">Lessons Learned / What's Next</p>
            <div class="content-block">
                <p>
                    The Monte Carlo sweeps were the clearest lesson from the
                    simulation side: intuition said servo speed would dominate
                    landing performance, and the data said otherwise. Sweeping
                    parameters individually before committing engineering time to
                    any one subsystem is now part of how I'd approach the next
                    project, not just this one.
                </p>
                <p>
                    From the static fires: the gimbal has clearly demonstrated
                    it has enough torque authority — it moved the full airframe
                    against gravity and against a mechanical stop, twice,
                    unintentionally. The open problems are precision and
                    correctness, not power. Fixes needed before the next fire:
                    invert the control signs, swap <code>write()</code> for
                    <code>writeMicroseconds()</code> with float math (dropping
                    ~2.6° steps to roughly 0.2°), verify true nozzle-neutral
                    against the assumed <code>OUTER_CENTER</code>, and add a
                    closed-loop sign-verification step to the pre-fire checklist
                    so this class of bug can't reach a live motor again.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Beyond the control loop: redesign the avionics bay for
                    accessibility, move to a barometric altimeter for
                    deployment, build one full CAD assembly with every part in
                    it before the next fire (rather than iterating on subsystems
                    in isolation), and reconsider the motor-casing attachment
                    for consistency and reusability. Open risks still being
                    tracked: motor thrust-to-weight margin, a control-loop
                    latency failure mode inherited from the reference build, and
                    whether the finished vehicle crosses from model-rocket into
                    high-power-rocketry classification, which would trigger
                    certification requirements.
                </p>
            </div>

            <footer>
                <p>Marcus David Yang</p>
